{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-334652" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The local truncation error (LTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}-(Δx)^{5}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.5,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.25,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.5$ to $Δx=0.25,$ the LTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070331/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f8639307f61ca296a9429a1925cbc7a1ad1f045d38fc20880ff40e8dc2e2056"} {"question" "q-334652" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the LTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.5,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.5) & =5(0.5)^{4}-(0.5)^{5} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0.062\\,5-1\\cdot 0.031\\,25 \\\\  & =0.281\\,25 \\\\  & ≈0.281.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.25,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.25) & =5(0.25)^{4}-(0.25)^{5} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0.003\\,90...-1\\cdot 0.000\\,97... \\\\  & =0.018\\,55... \\\\  & ≈0.019.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.5$ to $0.25.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.5}{0.25}=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={2}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.281\\,25$ to $0.018\\,55....$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.281\\,25}{0.018\\,55...}=15.157\\,89…≈15.158.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{p+1} & ≈15.157\\,89… \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{2}⁡(15.157\\,89…) \\\\ p+1 & ≈3.922… \\\\ p & ≈2.922….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$3.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the LTE:\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}-(Δx)^{5}\\,⇒\\,τ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{4})=O\\,((Δx)^{3+1}),\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070331/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f8639307f61ca296a9429a1925cbc7a1ad1f045d38fc20880ff40e8dc2e2056"} {"question" "q-334652" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070331/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7ce08aeeb11d94c29c390cfc34100b4d2ca03b5ef79f07200dad7f4a3990f0c9"} {"question" "q-334652" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070331/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f8639307f61ca296a9429a1925cbc7a1ad1f045d38fc20880ff40e8dc2e2056"} {"question" "q-334652" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.019"] ["math" "0.095"] ["math" "0.175"] ["math" "0.281"] ["math" "0.350"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070331/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f8639307f61ca296a9429a1925cbc7a1ad1f045d38fc20880ff40e8dc2e2056"} {"question" "q-334652" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.281"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070331/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f8639307f61ca296a9429a1925cbc7a1ad1f045d38fc20880ff40e8dc2e2056"} {"question" "q-334652" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070331/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f8639307f61ca296a9429a1925cbc7a1ad1f045d38fc20880ff40e8dc2e2056"} {"question" "q-334652" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.019"] ["math" "0.095"] ["math" "0.175"] ["math" "0.281"] ["math" "0.350"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070331/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f8639307f61ca296a9429a1925cbc7a1ad1f045d38fc20880ff40e8dc2e2056"} {"question" "q-334652" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.019"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070331/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f8639307f61ca296a9429a1925cbc7a1ad1f045d38fc20880ff40e8dc2e2056"} {"question" "q-334652" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070331/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f8639307f61ca296a9429a1925cbc7a1ad1f045d38fc20880ff40e8dc2e2056"} {"question" "q-334652" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12.640"] ["math" "15.158"] ["math" "4.500"] ["math" "6.250"] ["math" "9.375"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070331/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f8639307f61ca296a9429a1925cbc7a1ad1f045d38fc20880ff40e8dc2e2056"} {"question" "q-334652" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "15.158"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070331/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f8639307f61ca296a9429a1925cbc7a1ad1f045d38fc20880ff40e8dc2e2056"} {"question" "q-334652" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070331/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f8639307f61ca296a9429a1925cbc7a1ad1f045d38fc20880ff40e8dc2e2056"} {"question" "q-334652" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070331/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f8639307f61ca296a9429a1925cbc7a1ad1f045d38fc20880ff40e8dc2e2056"} {"question" "q-334652" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070331/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f8639307f61ca296a9429a1925cbc7a1ad1f045d38fc20880ff40e8dc2e2056"} {"question" "q-334709" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The local truncation error (LTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=4(Δx)^{3}-(Δx)^{4}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.3,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.1,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.3$ to $Δx=0.1,$ the LTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070331/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f8639307f61ca296a9429a1925cbc7a1ad1f045d38fc20880ff40e8dc2e2056"} {"question" "q-334709" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the LTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.3,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.3) & =4(0.3)^{3}-(0.3)^{4} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0.027-0.008\\,1 \\\\  & =0.108-0.008\\,1 \\\\  & =0.099\\,9 \\\\  & ≈0.100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.1,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.1) & =4(0.1)^{3}-(0.1)^{4} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0.001-0.000\\,1 \\\\  & =0.004-0.000\\,1 \\\\  & =0.003\\,9 \\\\  & ≈0.004.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.3$ to $0.1.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.3}{0.1}=3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={3}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.099\\,9$ to $0.003\\,9.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.099\\,9}{0.003\\,9}=25.615\\,38…≈25.615.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{p+1} & ≈25.615\\,38… \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{3}⁡(25.615\\,38…) \\\\ p+1 & ≈2.952… \\\\ p & ≈1.952….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$2.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the LTE:\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=4(Δx)^{3}-(Δx)^{4}\\,⇒\\,τ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{3})=O\\,((Δx)^{2+1}),\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070331/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f8639307f61ca296a9429a1925cbc7a1ad1f045d38fc20880ff40e8dc2e2056"} {"question" "q-334709" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070331/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7ce08aeeb11d94c29c390cfc34100b4d2ca03b5ef79f07200dad7f4a3990f0c9"} {"question" "q-334709" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070331/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f8639307f61ca296a9429a1925cbc7a1ad1f045d38fc20880ff40e8dc2e2056"} {"question" "q-334709" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.004"] ["math" "0.050"] ["math" "0.075"] ["math" "0.100"] ["math" "0.375"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070331/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f8639307f61ca296a9429a1925cbc7a1ad1f045d38fc20880ff40e8dc2e2056"} {"question" "q-334709" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math 